    "description": "A portable band saw is a different tool from a woodworking band saw: it is a handheld saw built for cutting pipe, conduit, threaded rod and steel stock on site. Milwaukee's M18 FUEL Deep Cut is the reference cordless model. Milwaukee lists the current version as the 2929-20, while many retailers still use the older 2729-20 number, and both are described as having a 5 by 5 inch cutting capacity. Supplyhouse and Milwaukee cite about 550 surface feet per minute for the 2929-20; Coastal Tool lists 380 for the older listing. Weight figures also differ, from 10.5 lb on one listing to about 15 lb on another, so check the exact model. The bare tool was listed at $379 to $399. For metal fabrication, plumbing and electrical work it is far quicker and cleaner than an angle grinder; for curves in wood, buy a benchtop or floor saw instead.",
    "pros": [
      "5 x 5 in cutting capacity per Milwaukee and retailers",
      "Cuts steel, pipe and conduit cleanly",
      "Runs on M18 batteries",
      "Bare tool listed at about $379 to $399"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not a woodworking band saw",
      "Model numbers 2729 and 2929 are mixed up between listings",
      "Listed weight varies from 10.5 to 15 lb"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If you cut metal stock on site rather than curves in wood, the M18 FUEL Deep Cut is the cordless portable band saw to buy."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Band Saws",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-band-saw/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best band saw for a home shop?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a home shop that wants to resaw, the Laguna 14|12, Rikon 10-326 and JET JWBS-14SFX are the strongest picks, all listed at 1-3/4 HP with 12 to 13 inches of resaw height. For curves and small stock, the WEN BA3962 costs about a quarter as much."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size band saw do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For resawing boards up to about 12 inches wide, buy a 14-inch saw with a rated resaw height of 12 inches or more. For curves in 3/4-inch stock, crafts and small parts, a 9 or 10-inch saw is enough. Throat capacity rarely limits home woodworking on a 14-inch saw."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much horsepower does a band saw need for resawing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most woodworkers find 1-3/4 HP comfortable for resawing hardwood at full height on a 14-inch saw. A 1 HP saw can resaw narrower boards with a sharp blade and slow feed but will struggle at full height in dense species."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a band saw cut metal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Woodworking band saws can cut some non-ferrous metals and plastics at slower speeds with the right blade, and two-speed saws help. Cutting steel needs much lower blade speeds, which is what portable metal-cutting band saws like the Milwaukee M18 FUEL Deep Cut are designed for."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a benchtop band saw worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A benchtop band saw is worth it for crafts, models, toys and thin stock if you have little space. It will not resaw thick boards, and its small motor limits it to light work. If you expect to grow into furniture making, a 14-inch saw is the better long-term buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What blade should I use for resawing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most woodworkers resaw with a 1/2 to 3/4 inch wide blade with 2 to 3 teeth per inch. Check the blade length your saw takes, because it varies widely between models, from 59-1/2 inches on the SKIL to 116 inches on the JET."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need dust collection for a band saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes for a 14-inch saw, which has a 4-inch port that needs a dust collector to clear chips well. Smaller saws with 1-1/2 to 2-1/2 inch ports usually work acceptably with a good shop vac."
          }
