          <SocialProof users="6.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#what-a-band-saw-does" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What It Does Best</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#resaw-and-throat" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Resaw & Throat</a>
          <a href="#power-and-speed" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Power & Speed</a>
          <a href="#blades" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Blades</a>
          <a href="#dust-and-space" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Dust & Space</a>
          <a href="#portable-band-saws" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Portable Saws</a>
          <a href="#which-saw" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Which Saw for You</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Safety</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The right band saw depends on one question: do you need to resaw thick boards? If you do, buy a 14-inch saw with 12 inches or more of height and at least 1-3/4 HP. If you mostly cut curves and small parts, a 10-inch or benchtop saw costs a fraction as much.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            These picks are researched from manufacturer specifications, retailer listings and published reviews rather than from our own shop test. Every resaw, throat, horsepower and weight figure is attributed to its source, and where Laguna, Rikon, JET, Grizzly, WEN, SKIL or Milwaukee disagree with a retailer, we say so. Resaw capacity is the spec that varies most, because some listings quote the distance under the upper guide and others quote the manufacturer's conservative rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            We grouped the saws by job rather than ranking one list. Three 14-inch floor saws cover serious resawing and furniture work. A cast-iron 14-inch saw covers curves at a lower price. A 10-inch saw and a 9-inch benchtop saw cover hobby and craft work. A portable cordless band saw covers metal cutting on site, which is a separate category that many shoppers search for under the same name.
          </p>

          <p>
            We favored saws that run on standard 110 to 120V household circuits, because most home shops do not have 220V. We also favored saws from brands with published spec sheets, parts and warranties, because band saws need blades, tires and guides replaced over their lives.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are new to band saws, the most important decision is not the brand. It is whether you need to resaw thick boards into thinner ones. Resawing needs a 14-inch saw with 12 inches or more of height and a strong motor. Cutting curves in 3/4-inch stock does not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-a-band-saw-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a Band Saw Does Better Than Other Saws
          </h2>

          <p>
            A band saw uses a continuous loop of thin blade running over two wheels, so the cut is always downward into the table. That makes it the safest saw for cutting curves, small parts and thick stock, because there is no kickback toward you in the way a <a href="/best-table-saw/">table saw</a> can kick back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its signature job is resawing: standing a board on edge and slicing it into thinner boards, which lets you make bookmatched panels, veneers and thin stock from thick lumber. No other common shop saw does this well. It is also excellent for cutting curves in thick stock that a <a href="/best-jigsaw/">jigsaw</a> would struggle with, and for rough-cutting joinery such as tenons.
          </p>

          <p>
            A band saw is not a replacement for a table saw when you need perfectly square, finish-ready rips, or for a <a href="/best-miter-saw/">miter saw</a> when you need accurate crosscuts. The cut surface usually needs planing or sanding, which is where a <a href="/best-benchtop-planer/">benchtop planer</a> earns its keep after resawing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many woodworkers add a band saw as their second or third big tool, after a table saw and a miter saw, once they want curves, resawing or safer ripping of small parts.
          </p>
