        <div id="resaw-and-throat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Resaw Height and Throat Capacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two numbers describe a band saw's capacity. Throat capacity is the distance from the blade to the frame, which sets the widest cut you can make. Resaw height is the distance from the table to the highest position of the upper blade guide, which sets the thickest board you can cut on edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most 14-inch saws have a throat of about 13-1/2 inches, and that is plenty for most furniture parts. Resaw height is where saws differ: the Rikon 10-326 and JET JWBS-14SFX list 13 inches, the Laguna 14|12 is rated at 12 inches by Laguna and 13 inches by some retailers, and the Grizzly G0555LX starts at 6 inches and reaches 12 inches with an optional riser block.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resawing a wide board also needs power. A 6-inch-wide maple board is far easier to resaw than a 12-inch board, and a 1 HP motor will struggle at full height in hardwood where a 1-3/4 HP motor will not. If resawing is your main goal, buy for height and horsepower together.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smaller saws have much less resaw height. The WEN BA3962 lists 6 inches, enough for small boxes and thin stock, and the SKIL 3386-01 cuts about 3-1/2 inches high, which limits it to crafts and thin material.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-and-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Motor Power, Circuits and Blade Speed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Horsepower decides how well a band saw resaws hardwood. The 14-inch floor saws here list 1 to 1-3/4 HP. The 1-3/4 HP saws from Laguna, Rikon and JET are the ones to buy if you resaw regularly. Smaller saws list amps instead: 3.5 amps for the WEN and 2.5 amps for the SKIL.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check your circuit before you buy. The Laguna 14|12 runs on 110V. The Rikon and JET run on 115V or 230V, and one retailer recommends a 20-amp circuit for the JET. If your saw will share a circuit with a dust collector, a 20-amp dedicated circuit is worth having an electrician add.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blade speed is listed in surface feet per minute. Around 3,000 feet per minute suits most wood. A lower second speed, offered by the Rikon, Grizzly and WEN, gives more control in tight curves and is useful for plastics and some non-ferrous metals. Cutting steel needs much lower speeds, which is what a portable metal band saw like the Milwaukee is designed for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Variable-speed and two-speed saws change speed by moving a belt between pulleys on most models. It is a quick job, but it is not a dial, so plan your cuts accordingly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Blades Make More Difference Than the Saw
          </h2>

          <p>
            The blade matters more to cut quality than almost any feature on the saw. Narrow blades, 1/8 to 1/4 inch wide, cut tight curves. Wide blades, 1/2 to 3/4 inch, track straighter for ripping and resawing. Fewer teeth per inch cut faster in thick stock; more teeth leave a smoother finish in thin stock.
          </p>

          <p>
            For resawing, most woodworkers use a 1/2 to 3/4 inch blade with 2 to 3 teeth per inch, often a hook or variable-tooth pattern. Grizzly notes that the G0555LX does not ship with a resaw blade, and JET lists its blade as sold separately, so budget for at least one good resaw blade on top of the saw price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the blade length your saw takes before ordering: listings give 115 inches for the Laguna 14|12, 111 inches for the Rikon 10-326, 116 inches for the JET, 93-1/2 inches for the Grizzly, 72 inches for the WEN and 59-1/2 inches for the SKIL. Blades of the wrong length will not tension.
          </p>

          <p>
            Proper setup, including blade tension, tracking and guide adjustment, fixes most problems people blame on the saw, such as drift and wavy resaw cuts. Spend an hour with the manual when the saw arrives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dust-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Dust Collection and Shop Space
          </h2>

          <p>
            Band saws make fine dust, especially when resawing. The 14-inch saws here have 4-inch dust ports, and the JET has two. A 4-inch port needs a real dust collector, not just a shop vac, to clear chips well. Our <a href="/best-dust-collector/">dust collector guide</a> covers the collectors sized for a small shop.
          </p>

          <p>
            For smaller saws with 1-1/2 to 2-1/2 inch ports, a good <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac</a> is usually enough. Our <a href="/shop-vac-vs-dust-extractor/">shop vac vs dust extractor guide</a> explains when a vac stops being enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 14-inch floor saw needs a permanent spot with room in front and behind for long boards, plus roughly 270 to 285 pounds of floor support, based on the listed weights. Mobile bases help in a shared garage. Benchtop saws can live on a shelf and come out for a project.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lighting matters too. A work light on the saw, as the WEN includes, makes it easier to follow a line, especially in a dim garage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="portable-band-saws" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Portable Band Saws Are a Different Tool
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search for a band saw and you will also see handheld portable band saws, such as Milwaukee's M18 FUEL Deep Cut. These are metalworking tools for cutting pipe, conduit, threaded rod, unistrut and steel stock on site. They cut cooler and cleaner than an <a href="/best-angle-grinder/">angle grinder</a> and with less vibration than a <a href="/best-reciprocating-saw/">reciprocating saw</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milwaukee lists a 5 by 5 inch capacity for its Deep Cut model. That is enough for most plumbing, electrical and light fabrication work. Some trades mount portable band saws in a table stand to make a small vertical saw for metal.
          </p>

          <p>
            A portable band saw is not suitable for woodworking curves or resawing. If you build furniture, buy a benchtop or floor saw. If you install pipe, conduit or strut, a portable saw is one of the most useful cordless tools you can own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery platform matters here as it does for every cordless tool. Our <a href="/best-cordless-tool-brand/">cordless tool brand guide</a> can help you choose a platform if you are starting from scratch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-saw" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Band Saw Fits Your Shop
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you build furniture and want to make your own thin stock, veneers or bookmatched panels, the decision is between the three 1-3/4 HP 14-inch saws. The Laguna 14|12 suits shops with only a 110V circuit, the Rikon 10-326 adds a second blade speed and a full 13 inches of listed resaw height, and the JET JWBS-14SFX adds a steel frame and dual dust ports. All three were listed in the $1,500 to $1,700 range when we checked, so availability and the warranty you prefer can reasonably decide it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you mostly cut curves for chairs, shelves, signs and templates, and only occasionally resaw narrow boards, the Grizzly G0555LX is the sensible middle ground. Its 1 HP motor and 6-inch stock height handle 3/4-inch and 8/4 stock comfortably, and the optional riser block lets you grow into resawing later without replacing the saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are starting out, have a small garage or only need a band saw a few times a year, the WEN BA3962 gives you real band saw capability, including a listed 6 inches of resaw height, for under $400 with a stand. The SKIL 3386-01 is smaller still and best for crafts, models, toys and thin stock where space is tighter than ambition.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your work is plumbing, electrical, HVAC or metal fabrication, skip the woodworking saws entirely and look at a cordless portable band saw like the Milwaukee M18 FUEL Deep Cut. It will cut pipe, conduit, threaded rod and strut faster and cleaner than the alternatives, and it runs on batteries you may already own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, set aside money for blades from day one. A good resaw blade, a narrow curve-cutting blade and a general-purpose blade in the correct length will do more for cut quality than any upgrade to the saw itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Band Saw Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Set the upper blade guide about 1/4 inch above the workpiece for every cut. That limits how much blade is exposed and gives the blade the most support. Use a push stick for narrow parts and keep your fingers out of the line of the blade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never back out of a long curved cut with the saw running, because that can pull the blade off the wheels. Turn the saw off, let it stop, and then back the workpiece out. Make relief cuts before cutting tight curves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Round stock, such as dowels and logs, can roll and grab the blade. Clamp it in a V-block or sled before cutting. Wear eye protection, and hearing protection when resawing for long periods.
          </p>

          <p>
            Disconnect power before changing blades, adjusting guides or tensioning, and release tension when the saw will sit unused for long periods if your manual recommends it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Too Little Resaw Height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you want to resaw, a 6-inch saw will frustrate you quickly. Buy a 14-inch saw rated for 12 inches or more, or one that accepts a riser block, and pair it with enough horsepower to drive a wide blade through hardwood.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the Saw for a Bad Blade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most wavy cuts and drift come from a dull or wrong blade, poor tension or misadjusted guides. Fit a proper resaw blade, tension and track it per the manual, and set the guides before deciding the saw is the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1-3/4 HP saw on a shared 15-amp circuit with a dust collector will trip breakers. Check the motor's amp draw and whether it runs on 115V or 230V, and consider a dedicated 20-amp circuit for the shop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing Portable and Woodworking Band Saws</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A handheld portable band saw is a metal-cutting tool. It will not resaw lumber or cut furniture curves. If you are buying for woodworking, buy a benchtop or floor saw.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Upper Guide Raised</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running with the upper guide high exposes more blade and lets it wander. Lower the guide to about 1/4 inch above the work for every cut for better accuracy and safety.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

