        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best band saw for a home shop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a home shop that wants to resaw, the Laguna 14|12, Rikon 10-326 and JET JWBS-14SFX are the strongest picks, all listed at 1-3/4 HP with 12 to 13 inches of resaw height. For curves and small stock, the WEN BA3962 costs about a quarter as much.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size band saw do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For resawing boards up to about 12 inches wide, buy a 14-inch saw with a rated resaw height of 12 inches or more. For curves in 3/4-inch stock, crafts and small parts, a 9 or 10-inch saw is enough. Throat capacity rarely limits home woodworking on a 14-inch saw.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much horsepower does a band saw need for resawing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most woodworkers find 1-3/4 HP comfortable for resawing hardwood at full height on a 14-inch saw. A 1 HP saw can resaw narrower boards with a sharp blade and slow feed but will struggle at full height in dense species.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a band saw cut metal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Woodworking band saws can cut some non-ferrous metals and plastics at slower speeds with the right blade, and two-speed saws help. Cutting steel needs much lower blade speeds, which is what portable metal-cutting band saws like the Milwaukee M18 FUEL Deep Cut are designed for.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a benchtop band saw worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A benchtop band saw is worth it for crafts, models, toys and thin stock if you have little space. It will not resaw thick boards, and its small motor limits it to light work. If you expect to grow into furniture making, a 14-inch saw is the better long-term buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What blade should I use for resawing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most woodworkers resaw with a 1/2 to 3/4 inch wide blade with 2 to 3 teeth per inch. Check the blade length your saw takes, because it varies widely between models, from 59-1/2 inches on the SKIL to 116 inches on the JET.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need dust collection for a band saw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes for a 14-inch saw, which has a 4-inch port that needs a dust collector to clear chips well. Smaller saws with 1-1/2 to 2-1/2 inch ports usually work acceptably with a good shop vac.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
